<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fitna of al-Andalus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1009">1009</a>–<a href="/years/1031">1031</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fitna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> was the civil war that destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> between 1009 and 1031. It began with a revolt against the Amirid regime and pitted rival Umayyad claimants, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> troops, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqaliba">Saqaliba</a> commanders and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammudid-dynasty">Hammudids</a> against one another, while Córdoba and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madinat-al-zahra">Madinat al-Zahra</a> were sacked. In 1031 the caliphate was abolished and al-Andalus fragmented into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kingdoms.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fitna-of-al-andalus.jpg" alt="Fitna of al-Andalus" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DaniCBP · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caliphate_of_C%C3%B3rdoba_under_Almanzor_-_1000_CE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii">al-Hakam II</a>, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-ii">Hisham II</a> succeeded as a child under the regency of his mother, Subh of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, but power passed to the chief of police Muhammad ibn Abi '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a>, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a>, who removed the chamberlain Ja'far al-Mushafi and then Subh. Ruling as chamberlain while the caliph was kept from public view, Almanzor founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amirids">Amirid dynasty</a>, a state within the state that favoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqaliba">Saqaliba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> and excluded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> from office and command. His son al-Muzaffar died in October 1008 and was succeeded by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-sanchuelo">Abd al-Rahman Sanchuelo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-sanchuelo">Sanchuelo</a> forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-ii">Hisham II</a> on 16 November 1008 to name him heir, angering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>. While he campaigned against Sancho García, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, the Umayyad prince Muhammad ibn Hisham, with commoners financed by al-Muzaffar's mother al-Zalfa', stormed the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> on 16 January 1009, deposed Hisham II and proclaimed himself Caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ii-of-cordoba">Muhammad II al-Mahdi</a>. Sanchuelo, abandoned by his soldiers, was killed on 3 March 1009. Al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>'s harsh treatment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> drove them to proclaim another Umayyad, Sulayman ibn al-Hakam, as caliph al-Musta'in.</p><p>Aided by the Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, Sulayman's Berbers defeated al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> at Qantish and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> on 8 September 1009, while al-Mahdi hired troops from Ramon Borrell, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, and Ermengol I, Count of Urgell. In July 1010 al-Mahdi was killed and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-ii">Hisham II</a> restored, ceding some two hundred border fortresses to Castile. The Berbers then besieged and sacked Córdoba and reinstated Sulayman, who gave provinces to his Berber allies, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> to Ali ibn Hammud.</p><p>Claiming that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-ii">Hisham II</a> had named him heir, Ali ibn Hammud took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> on 7 July 1016 and executed Sulayman. After Ali's assassination in 1018, his brother al-Qasim and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-ibn-ali">Yahya ibn Ali</a> contended for the throne with Umayyad candidates such as Abd al-Rahman IV, Abd al-Rahman V and Muhammad III, while the people of Córdoba rose repeatedly against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> garrisons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Cordobans chose the Umayyad Hisham ibn Muhammad, proclaimed al-Mu'tad on 15 June 1026, but power lay with his minister al-Hakam ibn Sa'id al-Qazzaz, who was assassinated in 1031. On 30 November 1031 they deposed Hisham III, abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> and banished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> from the city. The repeated looting of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> had destroyed monuments such as the Alcázar of the Caliphs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madinat-al-zahra">Medina Azahara</a>, the taxes of continuous war had brought financial collapse, and Christian kingdoms had used the conflict to annex border lands. Some successor kingdoms, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammudid-dynasty">Hammudid dynasty</a>, went on claiming the caliphate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fitna of al-Andalus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Fitna of al-Andalus</b>, a civil war of more than twenty years, followed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
